# Work-Spec Authoring

## Context

Work specs (`work/*.spec.edn`) are the unit of autonomous implementation
for miniforge: a well-formed work spec is sufficient for a miniforge
workflow (or a human-driven agent) to plan, implement, verify, review,
and release a coherent change. A poorly-formed work spec silently
wastes tokens and agent cycles.

This rule applies whenever an agent (or a human) creates or edits a
file matching `work/*.spec.edn`.

**Parent authority:** `foundations/specification-standards` (Dewey 020)
governs the normative specs this rule's work specs implement against.
This rule is scoped specifically to the work-spec authoring format.

## Required fields

<required>
Every `work/*.spec.edn` file MUST contain the following top-level keys:

- `:spec/title` — short imperative string
- `:spec/description` — full prose description of what + why + how
- `:spec/intent` — `{:type <keyword> :scope [<path-glob> ...]}`
  - `:type` MUST be one of `:refactor :feature :bugfix :general :chore
    :docs :test :performance`
  - `:scope` MUST be a vector of file paths or glob patterns, NOT a
    prose description
- `:spec/constraints` — vector of short constraint strings
- `:spec/acceptance-criteria` — vector of testable assertions
- `:spec/priority` — see below
- `:spec/workflow-type` — a keyword identifying the workflow chain
  (e.g., `:canonical-sdlc`)
</required>

## Required `:spec/priority` block

<required>
Every spec MUST carry:

```clojure
:spec/priority
{:tier       :blocker | :high | :medium | :low
 :axes       #{:dogfood-enabler :token-conservation :observation
               :correctness :scale :ux ...}   ; at least one
 :theme      <theme-id-keyword>                ; MUST exist in work/themes.edn
 :rationale  "One sentence — why this tier"
 :blocks     [<filename-string> ...]           ; specs this unblocks
 :blocked-by [<filename-string> ...]}          ; specs that must land first
```

Tier semantics:

| Tier | Use when |
|---|---|
| `:blocker` | Nothing above it moves until this lands. Costs real tokens or blocks release. Use sparingly. |
| `:high` | Next-in-line when blockers clear. |
| `:medium` | Default. Useful but not critical-path. |
| `:low` | Discretionary. |

Axis semantics:

| Axis | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `:dogfood-enabler` | Makes miniforge more capable of running miniforge |
| `:token-conservation` | Cuts cost per run |
| `:observation` | Improves what humans + automation can see |
| `:correctness` | Closes a correctness gap (bug, silent failure) |
| `:scale` | Enables larger deployment |
| `:ux` | Improves human-facing experience |

Pick all that apply — most specs are multi-axis.
</required>

## Required theme membership

<required>
`:spec/priority :theme` MUST be a key in `work/themes.edn`. A spec
referencing an unknown theme is rejected at review time.

Themes carry the authoritative `:theme/informative-spec` pointer + the
`:theme/normative-refs`. A spec inherits those anchors from its theme,
but MAY add spec-specific `:normative-refs` as well (see below).
</required>

## Normative anchor

<required>
Every work spec MUST have a normative anchor. Satisfied when EITHER:

- The theme has one or more `:theme/normative-refs`, OR
- The spec declares its own `:normative-refs` (map with
  `:primary` + optional `:secondary`, or a flat vector of strings —
  matches the existing `rn-*` pattern)

Specs with no normative anchor are rejected. This enforces the N-series
principle that implementation conforms to strategy, not the other way.
</required>

## Testable acceptance criteria

<required>
Each entry in `:spec/acceptance-criteria` MUST be a single assertion a
test or a human can check. Prefer criteria that become test names.

Good examples:

- `"poly check reports 0 errors on main"`
- `":workflow/dag-considered event emitted on every plan phase"`
- `"A killed run resumes with no re-run of successful phases"`

Bad examples (rejected at review):

- `"Implementation complete"` (not testable)
- `"System works"` (what does works mean?)
- `"Improves performance"` (by how much?)
</required>

## Invalid examples

<example type="invalid">
No priority block:

```clojure
{:spec/title "Fix thing"
 :spec/description "..."
 :spec/intent {:type :bugfix :scope ["..."]}
 :spec/acceptance-criteria ["Works better"]
 :spec/workflow-type :canonical-sdlc}
```

Issues: no `:spec/priority`, untestable acceptance criterion, no
normative anchor, no theme.
</example>

<example type="invalid">
Prose scope:

```clojure
:spec/intent {:type :feature
              :scope "Refactor the agent module and related areas"}
```

`:scope` MUST be a vector of file paths or globs — otherwise tooling
cannot compute affected bricks, change-classifiers cannot filter, and
the plan phase cannot decompose the change.
</example>

## Good example

See [templates/work-spec.edn.template](../templates/work-spec.edn.template) — a complete, valid spec showing all required fields.

## Agent behavior

When you create or modify a `work/*.spec.edn`:

1. Check `work/themes.edn` first — pick the existing theme that fits,
   or propose a new theme entry (with `:theme/informative-spec`) in the
   same PR
2. Set `:spec/priority :tier` honestly — do not inflate tier to jump
   the queue; the `bb work:queue` renderer reveals this to reviewers
3. Write acceptance criteria as testable assertions — one per line
4. Set `:scope` as file paths/globs, never prose
5. After editing, run `bb work:queue` to regenerate `work/QUEUE.md`
   and confirm your spec appears in the expected theme at the
   expected tier

When reviewing a work spec:

- If any required field is missing, reject
- If the theme doesn't exist, reject (or propose its addition)
- If acceptance criteria are untestable, reject
- If normative anchor is missing, reject
